        "direct_body_paragraph_zero_based": 565,
        "paragraph_id": "2D3EE986",
        "text": "In (2.58) the subscript s refers to the location at the surface. The dynamic pressure at the bed is subsequently solved by combining (2.57) and the local continuity equation:"
      }
    ],
    "following_nonempty_paragraphs": [
      {
        "container_path": "models/XBeach/raw/source_code/trunk/doc/manual/XBeach_manual_kingsday.docx",
        "member": "word/document.xml",
        "start_xml_ordinal": 23960,
        "body_paragraph_zero_based": 661,
        "direct_body_paragraph_zero_based": 569,
        "paragraph_id": "7262907A",
        "text": "In order to improve the computed location and magnitude of wave breaking, the hydrostatic front approximation (HFA) of Smit et al. (2010) is applied, in which the pressure distribution under breaking bores is assumed to be hydrostatic. Following the recommendations of Smit et al. (2010), we consider hydrostatic bores if  and reform if . The values can respectively be changed with the keywords maxbrsteep and secbrsteep."
      },
      {
        "container_path": "models/XBeach/raw/source_code/trunk/doc/manual/XBeach_manual_kingsday.docx",
        "member": "word/document.xml",
        "start_xml_ordinal": 24086,
        "body_paragraph_zero_based": 664,
        "direct_body_paragraph_zero_based": 572,
        "paragraph_id": "364F504F",
        "text": "Figure 2.8\tDuring wave breaking and wave runup the wave is modelled as a bore (Smit et al, 2010)"
      }
    ],
